y authentication secrets or network policies blocking access.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n\n\nThe result from the last attempt of mitigation is as follows:\n\n[False, [<clients.stratus.weak_oracles.base_oracle.OracleResult object at 0x7feb62e53620>]]\n\nThere are some reflections from the previous run:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by going through the conversation step by step. The user is dealing with a Kubernetes issue where the custom-service pods are stuck in the Init:0/1 state. The LLM is trying to figure out the root cause.\n\nFirst, the user provided a detailed setup, including the namespace and the faults identified. The LLM starts by checking metrics and then uses kubectl commands to inspect the pods. They tried getting metrics for user-service, which came back empty, indicating the service isn\'t running. Then they checked the custom-service deployment and found 0/3 replicas available.\n\nThe LLM then uses get_metrics to check for container status waiting reasons, but the result is empty. Next, they attempt to describe a specific pod but get a "Not Found" error. They list all pods and find the correct ones, then describe one of them, which shows the main container\'s image isn\'t pulling. The deployment YAML confirms the image is python:3.9-slim with IfNotPresent policy. The init container is just sleeping, so it doesn\'t help. The final answer points to the image pul